::STAT_U03_UnitAssessment_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U03_UnitAssessment_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.1
Question:
In item 1, A scatter plot compares hours of practice and contest score for a sample of students. Which response best applies correlation, regression models, residuals, and categorical association to support a statistically valid conclusion for the lesson topic: 
{
=A. Describe direction, strength, form, and context before making a conclusion. #Correct. This answer uses the lesson concept in context, connects the data or model to the question, and supports a valid statistical conclusion.
~B. Select the graph or rule that looks most familiar without checking the variables. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
~C. Use only the largest number in the data set as the conclusion. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
~D. Assume the result proves causation because two variables appear related. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
}

::STAT_U03_UnitAssessment_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U03_UnitAssessment_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.2
Question:
For item 2, A residual plot is used to decide whether a linear model fits a bivariate data set. What should a student do first to analyze the situation using correlation, regression models, residuals, and categorical association in the context of 
{
~A. Use only the largest number in the data set as the conclusion. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
=B. Use the residuals to evaluate whether the model fits the data well. #Correct. This answer uses the lesson concept in context, connects the data or model to the question, and supports a valid statistical conclusion.
~C. Assume the result proves causation because two variables appear related. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
~D. Ignore the context and report only a formula or isolated calculation. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
}

::STAT_U03_UnitAssessment_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U03_UnitAssessment_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.3
Question:
Item 3 asks about Unit 3 Synthesis. A table compares study format with whether students met a benchmark. Which interpretation is most appropriate when using 
{
~A. Assume the result proves causation because two variables appear related. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
~B. Ignore the context and report only a formula or isolated calculation. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
=C. Distinguish association from causation unless the study design supports causation. #Correct. This answer uses the lesson concept in context, connects the data or model to the question, and supports a valid statistical conclusion.
~D. Use the same method for every problem without checking conditions. #This is not the best answer. It skips an important statistical condition, context check, or evidence-based reasoning step needed for this lesson standard.
}

